    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.surround\n\n### Question\n\nNothing serious, just curious about the reason behind the decision. The 'b' keybind handles the balanced bracket for `()`, `{}`, and `[]`; but not `<>`. Why not include the angle bracket as well? ",
    "bodyHTML": "<h3 dir=\"auto\">Contributing guidelines</h3>\n<ul class=\"contains-task-list\">\n<li class=\"task-list-item\"><input type=\"checkbox\" id=\"\" disabled=\"\" class=\"task-list-item-checkbox\" aria-label=\"Completed task\" checked=\"\"> I have read <a href=\"https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\">CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Module(s)</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">mini.surround</p>\n<h3 dir=\"auto\">Question</h3>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Nothing serious, just curious about the reason behind the decision. The 'b' keybind handles the balanced bracket for <code class=\"notranslate\">()</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">{}</code>, and <code class=\"notranslate\">[]</code>; but not <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;&gt;</code>. Why not include the angle bracket as well?</p>",
    "bodyText": "Contributing guidelines\n\n I have read CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md\n\nModule(s)\nmini.surround\nQuestion\nNothing serious, just curious about the reason behind the decision. The 'b' keybind handles the balanced bracket for (), {}, and []; but not <>. Why not include the angle bracket as well?",
