            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Mostly because `<` and `>` are also quite widely used as a one-off comparison signs. Unlike `()`, `[]`, and `{}`, which actually act as brackets.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Mostly because <code class=\"notranslate\">&lt;</code> and <code class=\"notranslate\">&gt;</code> are also quite widely used as a one-off comparison signs. Unlike <code class=\"notranslate\">()</code>, <code class=\"notranslate\">[]</code>, and <code class=\"notranslate\">{}</code>, which actually act as brackets.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Mostly because < and > are also quite widely used as a one-off comparison signs. Unlike (), [], and {}, which actually act as brackets.",
